30. Convergence, Total Factor Productivity and the Augmented Solow Model
Paper: Economics of Growth and Development I (ECNCC302) | Unit: III | Topic: Absolute and conditional convergence; total factor productivity; augmented Solow model
Introduction
The Solow model predicts that poor countries should grow faster than rich ones. Whether the data supports this, and what explains differences in income, are central questions in empirical growth.
Absolute convergence
Absolute (unconditional) convergence says that, regardless of characteristics, poorer economies grow faster and catch up. It holds if all economies share the same steady state. Evidence: convergence among US states, Japanese prefectures and OECD countries (Barro and Sala-i-Martin), but not across the world: the cross-country scatter of initial income and growth is flat or positive (Baumol, De Long). Rich and poor have diverged (Pritchett's "Divergence, Big Time").
Conditional convergence
Economies converge to their own steady states, determined by saving rates, population growth, education and institutions. Holding these constant, countries further below their steady state grow faster. Barro regressions: growth = a - b ln(y_0) + controls; a significant negative b (about 2 per cent per year) after controls indicates conditional convergence. Convergence clubs (Quah) emerge: a poverty trap group and a rich group.
Total factor productivity (TFP)
TFP is the part of output growth not explained by growth in measured inputs, the Solow residual: g_Y = a g_K + (1-a) g_L + g_A. It captures technology, efficiency, institutions, organisation and measurement errors. Growth accounting finds TFP explains a substantial share of growth and an even larger share of cross-country differences in income levels (Hall and Jones; Easterly and Levine). Asian growth miracle debate: Young and Krugman argued that East Asian growth was input-driven (perspiration, not inspiration).
Augmented Solow model (Mankiw-Romer-Weil, 1992)
Adds human capital H to the production function: Y = K^a H^b (AL)^(1-a-b). Steady-state income depends on saving in physical capital, saving in human capital (schooling), and population growth. With a = b = 1/3, the model explains about 80 per cent of cross-country income variation and predicts a slower convergence speed, matching the data better. The elasticity of income with respect to the saving rate is larger.
Evaluation
Criticisms: omitted-variable and endogeneity problems, measurement of human capital, and the fact that TFP differences remain large. Still, the model demonstrates that human capital accumulation is a major driver.
Conclusion
Convergence is conditional, TFP differences are crucial, and including human capital improves the Solow framework, leading toward endogenous growth theories.